## 0. Reality Check (What's Actually True Today)

Before any marketing decision, the honest state:

**What works:**
- Base VS Code extension published on VS Code Marketplace (`karnatipraveen.lineagelens`, v1.1.0) and Open VSX (under review). Installs in VS Code, Cursor, Windsurf, VSCodium.
- v1.0.0 GitHub release shipped with 4 downloadable artifacts: Base VSIX, Lite zip, Plus zip, Max zip.
- Proxy now does **structured edit capture** (not text scraping) for Tier 1 agents: Claude Code (Anthropic tool_use), Codex CLI (OpenAI Responses API + apply_patch DSL), Gemini CLI (Google functionCall). 74 unit tests passing.
- Backend dashboard, MCP server, governance routes all functional.
- 23 backend tests passing; CI green on every push.

**What doesn't work yet:**
- No real-world end-to-end test of Lite quickstart with a fresh user.
- No demo video, no screenshots in README, no real user feedback.
- Cursor/Windsurf agent-mode capture is editor-only (no prompt/model — those tools route through proprietary backends).
- No Aider, Cline, Roo Code adapters yet (Tier 2 not started).
- Zero installs from cold inbound — installs to date are author/test installs only.

**What is overpromise risk:**
- "Captures every AI tool" — false. Captures Tier 1 cleanly, Cursor/Windsurf editor-only, Copilot CLI not at all.
- "Self-hosted with full audit trail" — true, but team features (RBAC, SSO) need polish before enterprise demos.
- "Risk scoring per insertion" — heuristic only; not a replacement for SAST.

The core promise that holds up under scrutiny:

> **A self-hosted provenance layer for AI-generated code. For agentic CLI tools (Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI), it captures the prompt, model, tool, file, old/new content, and accept/reject status. For VS Code editor tools, it captures the file changes locally. All data stays on your hardware.**

Do not claim more than that.

