## 10. Pricing Model (To Decide)

Currently the entire project is free / MIT. Three realistic monetization paths:

### Path A — Pure Open Source (no revenue)

Treat as a portfolio project. Build resume / job pipeline / consulting leads.
- Pro: zero infrastructure to maintain, no payment processing, no support SLAs.
- Con: no income from the work.
- Best for: if the goal is to land a senior dev role at a company doing AI tooling.

### Path B — Open Core (paid Plus/Max features)

Keep Base + Lite free. Charge for Plus/Max features.
- Free: Base extension, Lite single-container.
- Paid Plus ($30/dev/mo or $300/team-of-10/mo): Postgres, team dashboard, MCP, GitHub Actions integrations.
- Paid Max ($1000/year self-host + support): Neo4j lineage, compliance export, priority support.
- Pro: real revenue if 10+ teams adopt.
- Con: requires Stripe, billing, support email, license keys.
- Best for: if willing to commit 6 months past launch.

### Path C — Hosted SaaS (cloud Plus/Max)

LineageLens.cloud with a hosted Plus/Max tier.
- $15/dev/mo cloud Plus, $50/dev/mo cloud Max.
- Pro: subscription revenue, easier onboarding (no Docker required).
- Con: full SaaS ops burden — uptime, security, customer data, single-tenant infra.
- Best for: not yet. Wait until 10+ teams use the self-hosted version first.

**Recommendation for the next 90 days:** Path A (pure free). Build user base. Decide on B vs. C only after seeing real adoption signals (not vanity metrics — actual usage logs from people who aren't you).

